2. **Report:** `C:\CE_PLAN\areas\release-v3-colleagues-HUNT_CIVIL_LM_2026-10-12.md`. Findings are numbered HUNT-LM-01 to LM-52, with the top 10 in plain English at the top. It was committed as `af8d4638` and pushed to origin/main after `git fetch` and `merge --no-edit`, with no rebase and the file added by name.
3. **How the work was split:** five parallel readers covered schedule build and text, schedule update and Xref, Checks and QA, CDREPAIR, and legacy migration with details and X-Civil import. I re-read the code myself for LM-01, 02, 03, 04, 06, 09, 20 and 43.
4. **Duplicates:** two readers found the inlet-merge fault and the depth-refresh fault independently, so each is filed once. LM-17 is noted as the part of the R00 step-iron fix that never reached the refresh path, so it should be logged against that bug rather than as a new problem.

In summary: a read-only audit of the installed Civil 3.0.55 found 52 new problems across schedules, checks, repair and legacy import, about ten of them serious. The Pit Schedule is the weakest area, because it can silently change or misprint real design data. Nothing has been fixed or tested in AutoCAD, and no bug numbers have been assigned.
